course: dictionary
lesson: 14820
title: Quench
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To quench something means to satisfy it or put it out. Read the clues around the word. «After the long hike, Mia drank a big glass of water to quench her thirst.» Thirst is a need, and drinking water is what you do to stop being thirsty, so quench means to satisfy or stop it.
explain: «The firefighters sprayed the hay bales until the flames were quenched.» Water is sprayed and the flames are gone, so quenched means put out. Try the swap: «until the flames were put out» still makes sense, so you have found the meaning.
explain: Science uses quench in a special way. «The blacksmith heated the iron bar until it glowed, then quenched it in cold water.» The bar glowed because it was very hot, and cold water cools it fast. To quench hot metal means to cool it quickly so it becomes hard.
fact: To quench means to satisfy a thirst, to put out a fire, or to cool hot metal quickly.
fact: «Quench your thirst» means drink until you are no longer thirsty.
fact: To quench a fire is to put it out, often with water.
fact: In science and metalwork, quenching hot metal in water or oil cools it fast, which makes it hard.
fact: Quench is a verb: quench, quenches, quenched, quenching.
exercise: «After the long hike, Mia drank a big glass of water to quench her thirst.» What does quench mean here? | Warm up | Spill | *Satisfy, so she is no longer thirsty | Forget
exercise: The firefighters used hoses to quench the blaze. What does quench mean here? | Light up | Sweep away | Paint | *Put out
exercise: The blacksmith quenched the red-hot iron in water. What does quench mean in science? | *Cooled it quickly to make it hard | Melted it into a liquid | Stretched it out | Painted it bright
test: Which phrase means you are no longer thirsty? | *Quench your thirst | Count to ten | Clean your boots | Fill your bag
test: Which is the correct past tense of quench? | *quenched | quench | quenching | quenches
exam: A blacksmith quenches hot iron in water. Why does he do this? | To warm it up before it bends | To make it shiny and soft | To dry it out in the sun | *To cool it quickly so it becomes hard
